From cd181ec604f0c26e61f19942e330ae5b85dcfda5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: utzcoz <utzcoz@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 16:48:05 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] vr: support WebXR Layers with OpenXR on Linux

The Vulkan graphics binding kept one set of intermediate images for the
whole session, so it could only drive a single projection layer. Keep
them per layer instead, in the layer's graphics binding data, and let
the binding report layer support.

WebGL content is bottom-up and Vulkan swapchains are top-down. The
runtime flips projection layers, as it did for the base layer, and the
other layer types too if it supports
XR_FB_composition_layer_image_layout. Otherwise the binding flips those
while copying them into the swapchain. It does not support cube layers,
whose six faces its single intermediate image cannot hold.

WebXR Layers also requires the runtime to support cylinder, equirect2
and cube layers all at once. Few Linux runtimes do: SteamVR supports
none of them and Monado has no cube layers. So on Linux, offer the
feature whenever the binding supports layers, since projection and quad
layers are core OpenXR, and refuse to create a layer only when its type
is not supported. Blink then keeps that layer inactive. The other
platforms are unchanged.

Bug: 506004811
Change-Id: I02db6550ecd1126bcef4bf4130c3195d1c6c1656
